WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.122
In the last part, we noticed how a familiar

2
00:00:03.144 --> 00:00:06.767
routine can carry an assumption. Now we will add

3
00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:10.500
context without turning that context into a prediction about

4
00:00:10.522 --> 00:00:11.033
a person.

5
00:00:12.199 --> 00:00:16.977
The Minnesota Communities collection brings together history, place, contributions,

6
00:00:17.266 --> 00:00:20.754
experiences, and questions for work. The material can help

7
00:00:20.777 --> 00:00:23.932
you prepare, notice a possible access barrier, and find

8
00:00:23.954 --> 00:00:27.110
ideas for respectful questions. It does not tell you

9
00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:30.132
what an individual believes, what language they use, whom

10
00:00:30.154 --> 00:00:31.999
they trust, or what they want you to do.

11
00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:35.410
You may explore a brief that is relevant to

12
00:00:35.432 --> 00:00:38.966
your work or simply follow the example. You are

13
00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:42.766
not expected to read the entire collection. Each person

14
00:00:42.788 --> 00:00:46.010
and each situation deserves attention in its own right.

15
00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:50.869
Imagine that you are helping prepare the planning invitation.

16
00:00:50.891 --> 00:00:54.158
You open a community brief before discussing the invitation

17
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.091
with people affected by it. A passage describes a

18
00:00:58.113 --> 00:01:01.224
historical experience or a possible obstacle to using a

19
00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:04.336
public service. That gives you a reason to ask

20
00:01:04.358 --> 00:01:08.247
whether the current process creates a similar barrier. It

21
00:01:08.269 --> 00:01:10.513
does not establish that the person you will meet

22
00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:11.597
has encountered it.

23
00:01:12.097 --> 00:01:14.563
Look at where a statement comes from and when

24
00:01:14.586 --> 00:01:17.719
it was written. A statistic may describe a particular

25
00:01:17.741 --> 00:01:21.986
place, period, definition, or group of respondents. A book

26
00:01:22.008 --> 00:01:25.497
offers an author's perspective. A community account may describe

27
00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:28.586
a real experience without representing every person in that

28
00:01:28.608 --> 00:01:31.941
community. If the source or its date is unclear,

29
00:01:32.341 --> 00:01:35.430
record the uncertainty and seek a current, appropriate source

30
00:01:35.452 --> 00:01:36.786
before repeating the claim.

31
00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:40.108
You do not have to turn every unknown into

32
00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.552
a question for the person receiving a service. Ask

33
00:01:43.574 --> 00:01:46.352
only what helps with the work. Do not ask

34
00:01:46.374 --> 00:01:50.619
someone to explain their community, faith, immigration history, or

35
00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:53.908
a painful experience simply to satisfy your curiosity.

36
00:01:55.142 --> 00:01:59.676
Suppose a brief discusses relationships and support. A rushed

37
00:01:59.698 --> 00:02:02.609
conclusion would be, “This person will want their family

38
00:02:02.631 --> 00:02:06.053
to decide.” A better question is, “Is there anyone

39
00:02:06.076 --> 00:02:08.187
you would like involved, and how would you like

40
00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:11.609
them to participate?” The question leaves room for a

41
00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:15.276
relative, a friend, a professional supporter, or no one

42
00:02:15.298 --> 00:02:15.587
else.

43
00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:19.877
The same habit applies to language. A person's name,

44
00:02:20.032 --> 00:02:23.432
appearance, community, or country of origin does not tell

45
00:02:23.454 --> 00:02:27.166
you which language or communication method they prefer. Make

46
00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:30.099
it easy for everyone to request support, and ask

47
00:02:30.121 --> 00:02:32.921
what works for the individual when that information is

48
00:02:32.943 --> 00:02:33.299
needed.

49
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:37.822
There is variety within every community, and people may

50
00:02:37.844 --> 00:02:41.244
identify with more than one community or with none

51
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:44.222
of the labels in a directory. Let the person

52
00:02:44.244 --> 00:02:47.844
describe what matters to them. When the brief raises

53
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:50.956
a question about the way your team works, examine

54
00:02:50.978 --> 00:02:53.378
the process as well as the conversation.

55
00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:57.893
Culture is not the only possible explanation for a

56
00:02:57.916 --> 00:03:02.871
barrier. A short response period, inaccessible document, missing interpretation,

57
00:03:03.293 --> 00:03:08.293
unreliable technology, transportation, workload, or unclear decision process may

58
00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:11.516
also affect participation. Start with what you can observe

59
00:03:11.538 --> 00:03:14.249
and the information people are willing to share; do

60
00:03:14.271 --> 00:03:16.316
not use a cultural label as a shortcut.

61
00:03:16.993 --> 00:03:20.460
In the planning example, the team might learn that

62
00:03:20.482 --> 00:03:23.838
a notice is difficult to use. The useful follow-up

63
00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.927
is to ask what made it difficult, what would

64
00:03:26.949 --> 00:03:30.860
help, and who can review a change. Explain what

65
00:03:30.882 --> 00:03:33.682
is open to change and how feedback will be

66
00:03:33.704 --> 00:03:37.327
considered. If an answer is not yet known, say

67
00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:39.367
so and identify a next step.

68
00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:44.800
For substantive questions concerning Native American individuals, communities, or

69
00:03:44.822 --> 00:03:48.844
Tribal Nations, follow this program's standing deference to the

70
00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:52.289
Office of Indian Affairs and the DHS offices responsible

71
00:03:52.311 --> 00:03:55.978
for Tribal relations and consultation. A general reading or

72
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.222
listening activity does not replace a Nation's authority or

73
00:03:59.244 --> 00:04:03.533
government-to-government consultation. Seek the appropriate guidance rather than inventing

74
00:04:03.556 --> 00:04:04.089
an answer.

75
00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:08.100
A colleague reads that some people may value involving

76
00:04:08.122 --> 00:04:12.389
trusted supporters. The colleague proposes inviting a participant's relatives

77
00:04:12.411 --> 00:04:16.344
to the planning discussion before asking the participant. What

78
00:04:16.367 --> 00:04:17.211
would you do first?

79
00:04:18.022 --> 00:04:21.400
Pause and choose an answer in the activity. Then

80
00:04:21.422 --> 00:04:25.067
read the explanation. Notice the difference between preparing an

81
00:04:25.089 --> 00:04:28.044
inclusive option and deciding for someone who should be

82
00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:28.556
involved.

83
00:04:30.572 --> 00:04:34.172
Choose one sentence or idea from an available brief,

84
00:04:34.194 --> 00:04:37.728
or use the example you just heard. Write down

85
00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.550
what it helps you notice, one question you could

86
00:04:40.572 --> 00:04:43.639
ask about the actual work, and one thing you

87
00:04:43.661 --> 00:04:46.906
do not yet know. Keep any note general and

88
00:04:46.928 --> 00:04:49.617
free of identifying or confidential information.

89
00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:53.544
You can follow the source links or an existing

90
00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:57.011
reading for deeper context. Treat the source as a

91
00:04:57.033 --> 00:05:01.033
starting point, not a substitute for listening. Next we

92
00:05:01.056 --> 00:05:03.856
will use the same approach to make communication and

93
00:05:03.878 --> 00:05:05.411
participation more accessible.
